      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Standing seam metal and high-wind-rated architectural asphalt shingles are the best roofing materials for Utah's extreme temperature swings, heavy snow loads, and high canyon winds.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Standing seam metal and high-wind-rated architectural asphalt shingles"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Top Roofing Materials for Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Standing Seam Metal / Stone-Coated Steel: This is the top choice for longevity. It easily sheds heavy mountain snow, resists high winds, handles freeze-thaw cycles without cracking, and lasts 40 to 70 years .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Architectural Asphalt Shingles (Class 3 or 4 Impact Rated): This is the most popular, budget-friendly option. Look for wind ratings of at least 110\u2013130 mph (such as GAF Timberline HDZ) to handle Utah's severe wind corridors along the Wasatch Front. They typically last 20 to 30 years.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Synthetic Shake: A great alternative to traditional wood cedar in mountain areas. It gives a rustic look, handles severe weather, offers high fire resistance, and requires less maintenance than natural wood."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Utah Weather Factors to Consider"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Wind: Utah experiences violent wind storms and canyon gusts. Ensure your installation includes high-wind nailing patterns and starter strips rated past 120 mph ."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Snow and Ice: Heavy snow loads require strong structural support, especially if considering heavy tile. Metal roofs allow snow to slide off cleanly, preventing dangerous buildup and ice dams.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Thermal Cycling: Utah's rapid shifts between freezing winter lows and intense summer heat stress materials. Proper attic ventilation and high-temperature ice-and-water shields are critical regardless of the material you choose."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow down the best choice for your home, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What is your approximate budget?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Are you located in a high-wind canyon area or heavy snow zone?"
          }
        ]
      }
